comments:
It appears that this may be due to how `$.update` works. The run function is compiled to the following code:
```js
run(() => {
	($.get(number), $.update(changes));
});
```
 In the `$.update` function, it runs `$.get` to get the initial value of the state before incrementing it. Since the `$.get(changes)` call is technically running inside a side effect function, `changes` gets added to the list of dependencies. 
 I think the best way to solve this would be to have an internal boolean that indicates whether the currently running `$.get` is running as a result of a `$.update` call, either in `$.get`'s arguments or as a variable.

In short, Svelte 4 has the following reactivity lifecycle:
1. looping over each `$:` block and running the ones that depend on dirtied variables;
2. running `beforeUpdate`s;
3. marking all variables as up to date;
4. updating DOM;
5. running `afterUpdate`s.

Step 3 prevents infinite loops but can also cause a loss of reactivity in some cases. There is no equivalent of this in Svelte 5.

---

The correct way to migrate the example would be
```js
run(() = {
  number;
  untrack(() => changes++);
});
```

But `$: number++;` in Svelte 4 won't cause an infinite loop, just one incrementation to each change of `number`.
In Svelte 5 this would look like
```js
let ran = false;
$effect(() => {
  if (ran) {
    number; // keep it as a dependency
    ran = false;
  } else {
    number++;
    ran = true;
  }
});
```
It can be generalized to
```js
looplessRun(
  () => [number], // dependencies
  () => { number++ }, // untracked code
);
```
and used in cases with potential self-triggering, like these examples.

A more tricky case is multiple reactive blocks triggering each other: maybe `ran` should be shared or reset after `tick`.
